From e70e93472b7ee3c962b3f5f20b58f08bf6e711cd Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "iLinux local research (AI-generated draft)" <local@invalid>
Date: Sun, 11 Oct 2026 20:04:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 5/7] nvme-apple: Support the M4+ NVMMU/NVMe register split
 (t8132, t6050)

From M4 (t8132) on, the ANS ADT node has an "nvme-secure-bar" property
and ten reg entries.  The NVMMU registers (TCB count, TCB bases, TCB
invalidation) stay in the window that held everything on M1-M3 (ADT
reg[3]) while the NVMe controller registers, doorbells, boot status and
the linear-SQ controls are accessed through ADT reg[9].  In addition,
after the I/O queues have been created the I/O CQ and SQ base addresses
have to be written to nvme+0x1208 and nvme+0x1200 (completion queue
first), otherwise the ANS firmware crashes and its crash log reports
I/O SQ/CQ = 0.

Add an "nvmmu" MMIO window for apple,t8132-nvme-ans2 (and SoCs that use
it as fallback, e.g. t6050), route all APPLE_NVMMU_* accesses through
it (it aliases the "nvme" window on older SoCs, so nothing changes
there), mirror the I/O queue bases on M4+, and handle three further
M4+ differences:

 - TCB invalidation status: M4+ keeps a per-TCB state array at the start
   of the NVMMU block (one 32-bit word per four TCBs, a 5-bit slot per
   TCB, low four bits zero once invalidated) instead of the single
   status register at 0x28120 read on M1-M3.  Check the slot of the tag
   that was just invalidated.
 - The 64-bit queue and TCB base registers (ASQ/ACQ, the two NVMMU TCB
   bases, the I/O queue bases) are programmed as two 32-bit stores, low
   word first, on M4+; older SoCs keep the 64-bit store.
 - The NVMMU TCB count register keeps the value 63 on M4+ whatever the
   queue depth, so max_queue_depth stays at 64.

This builds on the macOS 15+ firmware-ABI series already in the asahi
branch (Sven Peter, b4/nvme-fwabi; asahi commits 9c02d723ef80,
07133e8a2fb8, 835cbc4205ac, 055df6031321, c3d4bb4f1cab, a4f6716c6a69),
which is required as well: no opcode in the TCB, DMA direction only for
data transfers, page-aligned admin buffers, and no access to the
removed PRP-null-check register at 0x24008 (SErrors on new firmware).

Provenance: the window split and the I/O queue base writes follow m1n1
src/nvme.c, commit 53f8ee9b54ba ("nvme: support T8132", Yureka,
2026-07-26, AsahiLinux/m1n1 PR #652), MIT-licensed at tag v1.9.9
(commit 809541515659); m1n1 PR #661 states that with this and SARTv4,
nvme_init()/nvme_read() work on t6050.  The per-TCB status array, the
fixed TCB count value and the 32-bit store sequence were recovered by
offline analysis (REA/Ghidra) of the SPTM firmware of a Mac17,6 running
macOS 26.6.2 (iLinux kernel/experiments/nvme-ans3/README.md, section
4.1); the code here is original and written from those facts.  Because
of that analysis this patch cannot meet Asahi's clean-room policy.

Open questions (need native hardware): the ADT sizes reg[9] as 0x10000
but the linear-SQ registers at +0x24908..+0x24910 are accessed through
it (the t6050 draft DT maps 0x40000; macOS reaches them through
reg[3]); ADT nvme-queue-entries is 253 but the 64-tag limit is kept;
whether the M5 windows also accept 64-bit stores is unknown, hence the
32-bit sequence.

Status: untested on hardware; offline compile check in
virtualization/jobs/043-nvme-smc-patches.

NOT FOR UPSTREAM SUBMISSION: AI-generated local research draft (Asahi
Linux generative-AI policy).  No Signed-off-by on purpose.

